<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexei Brusilov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1853">1853</a>–<a href="/years/1926">1926</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexei Brusilov was a Russian general in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. His offensive of 1916 against Austro-Hungarian forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> inflicted very heavy losses. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brusilov-offensive">Brusilov Offensive</a> severely weakened the military of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>, which became increasingly dependent on German support.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexei-brusilov.jpg" alt="Alexei Brusilov" width="288" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%91%D1%80%D1%83%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2_%D0%90%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%B9_%D0%90%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aleksei Alekseyevich Brusilov was born on 31 August 1853 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a>, into a family in which three generations had served as officers in the Imperial Russian Army. His father, a lieutenant general, died of tuberculosis in 1856, and his Polish mother died soon after; he was raised by relatives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a>. He entered the Imperial Corps of Pages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> in 1867 and in 1872 was posted as an ensign to the 15th (Tver) Dragoon Regiment, stationed near Kutaisi. He served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877-78 and was decorated for the assault on the fortress of Ardagan and for attacks around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1881 Brusilov was at the Cavalry Officer School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, which he commanded from 1902 and made a recognised centre for training cavalry staff officers. He then commanded the 14th Army Corps in the Warsaw Military District from 1908 and the 12th Army Corps in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> from 1913. In July 1914 he took command of the 8th Army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, which crushed the Austro-Hungarian Third Army and advanced nearly 150 kilometres before retreating with the rest of the front. In the Great Retreat of 1915 the 8th Army withdrew 180 kilometres.</p><p>On 29 March 1916 he was given the Southwest Front. Earlier Russian offensives had struck ever narrower sections of the line. Brusilov instead attacked along the whole front, using fire on command posts and roads rather than saturation bombardment. Over three months the front advanced an average of more than 30 kilometres along more than 400 kilometres and took 450,000 Austro-Hungarian prisoners, but the supporting attack from the West Front was not delivered and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> moved 17 divisions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> to halt it.</p><p>He was one of eight Russian commanders awarded the Sword of Saint George with Diamonds. After asking for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a>'s abdication in 1917 under pressure from General Mikhail Alekseyev, he became Commander in Chief on 4 June 1917 and was replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavr-kornilov">Lavr Kornilov</a> after the failure of the Kerensky Offensive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Brusilov sided with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. In May 1920 he published an appeal in Pravda to former officers to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, and he served on a commission on its size and structure before becoming Inspector of Cavalry. He retired in 1924 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 17 March 1926 of congestive heart failure; he was buried in the Novodevichy Convent. His memoirs appeared in English in 1930 as A Soldier's Notebook, 1914-1918. Many pro-tsarist historians avoided praising him because of his service in the Red Army, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-denikin">Anton Denikin</a> portrayed him as having suffered a psychological breakdown during the 1916 offensive. Bernard Montgomery counted him among seven outstanding fighting commanders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aleksei Brusilov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Brusilov Offensive of June to September 1916, launched by the Russian general <b>Alexei Brusilov</b> along a front of several hundred kilometres, inflicted well over half a million Austro-Hungarian casualties, including very large numbers of prisoners, a blow from which the army never fully recovered.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.72,44.79&amp;year=1900" aria-label="Tbilisi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5641 677 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alexei Brusilov"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6091 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6091 947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.72,44.79&amp;year=1900">What was here in 1900? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
